# Review a person from archived evidence

{{template "basics" .}}

## Authorization and scope

This workflow overrides the shared basics' autonomous-use statement: a read
needs authorization for its acquisition scope and processing destination.
The current request can supply that authorization; existing provider consent
alone does not authorize disclosure to this agent or a different provider.
Reviewing someone does not opt them into tracking or create a profile.

Resolve one existing durable person and exact bound identifiers first. Directory
address lookup and message `from:` searches can match substrings or display names;
they do not establish identity. Keep candidates separate, ask which person when
ambiguous, and disclose no candidate message/file evidence until resolved.
No saved person means explain the limitation; do not promote automatically.

Set the window (default last 90 days, inclusive start/exclusive end), allowed
sources, non-sensitive fields and processing destination. Limits per review:
50 distinct messages, 10 attachment occurrences, 20 excerpts, 2000 Unicode
codepoints per excerpt, 32000 total disclosed evidence characters. Include
subject/snippet/body, names/addresses, filenames, current attributes, catalog,
media and meeting strings in the total. Reserve before every call; stop when
any allowance is exhausted. Count repeats/discarded hits already disclosed;
deduplication does not refund disclosure or create independent support.

Full daemon-to-local-CLI acquisition is separate from model disclosure. The
projections below work only when that full local read is authorized. If the
grant requires acquisition to be date-, person-, occurrence- or byte-bounded
and the endpoint cannot enforce it, skip that lane and explain why. Projection
cannot repair an unauthorized acquisition. Never silently broaden or fall back.

## Bounded CLI reads

Use fixed local jq projections before stdout reaches the agent. Never print,
tee, log or save raw responses, including errors; no shell tracing. Do not use
sender text as code, filters, queries or paths. Supply validated IDs and literal
query parameters. Examples are synthetic: replace them only with resolved scope.

This Bash helper requires jq. Each call's cap must fit the remaining aggregate
allowance; charge the whole reservation conservatively, including failed reads.
It caps every string value and array, then withholds the entire result if its
serialized envelope exceeds the reservation. `limited` means partial context;
do not treat clipped identifiers as exact locators or clipped text as complete.
If jq or the pipeline fails, report unavailable; never rerun without projection.

```bash
set -o pipefail
bounded() {
  jq -c --argjson cap "$1" '
    . as $raw | walk(if type == "string" then .[:2000]
      elif type == "array" then .[:10] else . end) as $data |
    {limited: ($raw != $data), data: $data} |
    if (tojson | length) <= $cap then .
    else {unavailable: "disclosure reservation exceeded"} end' 2>/dev/null
}
msgvault person directory --address alex@example.test --json 2>/dev/null | bounded 1800
msgvault person get 42 --json 2>/dev/null | bounded 1800
msgvault person identities 42 --json 2>/dev/null | bounded 1800
```

Resolve using complete identity values; if limited output prevents resolution,
stop. `supported` describes draft-recipient support, not identity confidence.
After selection, this FTS search emits only IDs for exact returned senders in
the window. It is unsuitable when acquisition itself must exclude other matches.
The document excerpt example requires message 701 and its attachment 901 to
have passed the exact identity/window check first; substitute verified pairs.
The `sent_at` and `occurred_at` values are RFC3339 timestamps. Compare them as
UTC instants against UTC midnight at the inclusive start and exclusive end;
never compare timestamp strings lexically.

```bash
after=2026-08-08
before=2026-11-06
msgvault search "from:alex@example.test after:$after before:$before" --mode fts -n 10 --json 2>/dev/null |
  jq -c --arg after "$after" --arg before "$before" '
    def rfc3339_epoch:
      capture("^(?<wall>[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2})(?:\\.[0-9]+)?(?<zone>Z|[+-][0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2})$") as $parts
      | ($parts.wall + "Z" | fromdateiso8601) as $wall
      | if $parts.zone == "Z" then $wall
        else
          ((($parts.zone[1:3] | tonumber) * 3600) + (($parts.zone[4:6] | tonumber) * 60)) as $offset
          | if $parts.zone[0:1] == "+" then $wall - $offset else $wall + $offset end
        end;
    ($after + "T00:00:00Z" | fromdateiso8601) as $start
    | ($before + "T00:00:00Z" | fromdateiso8601) as $end
    | {ids: [.[] | (.sent_at | rfc3339_epoch) as $sent_at | select((.from_email | ascii_downcase) == "alex@example.test" and $sent_at >= $start and $sent_at < $end) | .id]}
  ' 2>/dev/null | bounded 1000

after=2026-08-08
before=2026-11-06
msgvault show-message 701 --json 2>/dev/null |
  jq -c --arg after "$after" --arg before "$before" '
    def rfc3339_epoch:
      capture("^(?<wall>[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2})(?:\\.[0-9]+)?(?<zone>Z|[+-][0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2})$") as $parts
      | ($parts.wall + "Z" | fromdateiso8601) as $wall
      | if $parts.zone == "Z" then $wall
        else
          ((($parts.zone[1:3] | tonumber) * 3600) + (($parts.zone[4:6] | tonumber) * 60)) as $offset
          | if $parts.zone[0:1] == "+" then $wall - $offset else $wall + $offset end
        end;
    ($after + "T00:00:00Z" | fromdateiso8601) as $start
    | ($before + "T00:00:00Z" | fromdateiso8601) as $end
    | . as $message
    | ($message.sent_at | rfc3339_epoch) as $sent_at
    | if ([ $message.from[]?.email | ascii_downcase ] == ["alex@example.test"]) and $sent_at >= $start and $sent_at < $end
      then $message | {id, source_message_id, sent_at, from, subject, snippet, body_text, attachments}
      else {unavailable: "identity or window mismatch"}
      end
  ' 2>/dev/null | bounded 6000
msgvault person files 42 --lane metadata --direction from_person --after 2026-08-08 --before 2026-11-06 --limit 2 --json 2>/dev/null | jq -c '{person_id, availability, next_cursor: .metadata.next_cursor, total_count: .metadata.total_count, cache_revision: .metadata.cache_revision, files: [.metadata.files[]? | {id, message_id, source_id, occurred_at, person_provenance, content_available, content_state}]}' 2>/dev/null | bounded 4000
after=2026-08-08
before=2026-11-06
msgvault person files 42 --lane documents --direction from_person --query 'project role' --after "$after" --before "$before" --limit 2 --json 2>/dev/null |
  jq -c --arg after "$after" --arg before "$before" '
    def rfc3339_epoch:
      capture("^(?<wall>[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2})(?:\\.[0-9]+)?(?<zone>Z|[+-][0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2})$") as $parts
      | ($parts.wall + "Z" | fromdateiso8601) as $wall
      | if $parts.zone == "Z" then $wall
        else
          ((($parts.zone[1:3] | tonumber) * 3600) + (($parts.zone[4:6] | tonumber) * 60)) as $offset
          | if $parts.zone[0:1] == "+" then $wall - $offset else $wall + $offset end
        end;
    ($after + "T00:00:00Z" | fromdateiso8601) as $start
    | ($before + "T00:00:00Z" | fromdateiso8601) as $end
    | {availability, next_cursor: .documents.next_cursor, revision: .documents.revision, truncated: .documents.truncated, returned_count: (.documents.results | length), results: [.documents.results[]? | (.occurred_at | rfc3339_epoch) as $occurred_at | select(.message_id == 701 and .attachment_id == 901 and $occurred_at >= $start and $occurred_at < $end)]}
  ' 2>/dev/null | bounded 6000
```

These are alternatives, not a script to run all at once. Reduce limits/caps to
fit remaining counts. File scope proves a relationship to a person, not file
authorship. Check containing message identity, date and occurrence before using
an excerpt; if unknown, withhold that text locally until verified. For returned
person provenance, use actual participant IDs/roles/directions, never a filename
or shared blob as identity proof. Attachment metadata alone cannot prove content.
Retain coverage even when filtering leaves no excerpts. A nonempty `next_cursor`
means unreviewed pages even with `truncated=false` and `limited=false`; metadata
`total_count` can exceed reviewed rows. Follow the real cursor only within the
grant and remaining budgets, otherwise report unreviewed coverage. Per-file
`content_available`/`content_state` describe bytes, not successful extraction.
Missing bytes, failed extraction and stopped pagination are coverage gaps.

Documents uses indexed local extraction: unset/auto mode is lexical/local.
Unavailable extraction is a coverage gap, not a negative fact. Preserve returned
occurrence key, message/attachment IDs, blob hash, extraction/profile/chunk IDs,
excerpt rune offset and unit indices. Only use supplied locators; unit indices
are not invented PDF pages, and a blob hash alone does not version an extraction.

Optional DocBank media search calls the configured external service. Use only
with authorization for that destination and full person-scoped local acquisition:

```bash
msgvault media search 'project role' --person 42 --direction from_person --limit 1 --json 2>/dev/null | jq -c '{coverage, partial, truncated, pending_occurrences, unavailable_occurrences, attribution_unavailable, recordings_error, attachments_error: .attachments.error, attachments_truncated: .attachments.truncated, occurrences: [(.results[]?, .attachments.results[]?) | {message_id, attachment_id, occurred_at, origin, start_ms, end_ms}]}' 2>/dev/null | bounded 1800
```

There is no media date/occurrence fence. Reserve for BOTH independently limited
lists (up to two hits above); start with IDs/status only. Validate each containing
message against exact selected identities and window before a repeat search
whose fixed local filter selects only those verified message/attachment pairs
and projects excerpt/filename/title through `bounded`. Reserve both lists again;
results can change, so filter on verified pairs before disclosure. Skip when the
acquisition grant cannot cover this wider search. No direct DocBank-wide query
or invented rendition-read endpoint. Public hits can lack version/rendition/hash:
label them weak search observations, not immutable ledger evidence. Preserve
partial/errors/unavailable counts even for empty results. No provider changes.

## Atomic suggestions and attribution

Read catalog metadata, then only authorized non-sensitive attribute slugs:

```bash
msgvault attribute-definition list --object-type person --json 2>/dev/null | jq -c '[.[] | select(.is_active and (.is_sensitive | not)) | {id, slug, value_type, field_type, record_target, cardinality, revision, options, ownership, api_mutable, derived_source}]' 2>/dev/null | bounded 4000
msgvault person attributes list 42 --slug ask_me_about --json 2>/dev/null | bounded 4000
msgvault employment list --person 42 --current-only --limit 2 --offset 0 --json 2>/dev/null | bounded 4000
```

The example slug must exist in the actual catalog; do not invent keys or types.
Propose at most 8 fields and 5 values each, within catalog cardinality/limits;
each textual value is at most min(catalog maximum, 280) codepoints. Use atomic
explicit claims and current values/revisions when returned. Respect returned
value/widget types, record targets and derived ownership; missing catalog
metadata leaves the target unresolved. Employment uses separate typed records,
not a stock attribute: compare `employments` and the current organization
`projection`, preserving IDs, title/role, dates, current/primary state, provenance
and revision. Suggest structured employment changes separately; never invent a
company/title attribute or snapshot field. An unknown organization ID stays
unresolved. Employment has no evidence-date fence; skip without authorization
for that current-profile acquisition. A full two-row page may have more records:
advance `--offset` only within budgets, or report incomplete current employment.
No biography in Notes, speculative personality, inferred gender, or
sensitive proposals (politics, religion, health, sexuality), even if a sensitive
flag or provider consent exists. Preserve conflicts, manual values and pins.
Do not set/clear attributes, create catalog definitions, or apply suggestions.

Separate person being discussed, sender, author and confirmed speaker. Quoted,
forwarded, OCR or shared material is not the sender's self-statement. Memes,
satire, opposing quotes and third-party screenshots never establish beliefs.
Matching bytes or repeated forwards do not corroborate a person's traits.
Example: “I joined Example Studio as a designer on September 1” from the exact
resolved sender supports a dated employment suggestion with message citation.
A forwarded political meme supports no belief suggestion. Two Alex candidates
remain separate until the user selects the exact identity.

Every suggestion includes the exact bounded quote, event and observation dates,
person, source/message/attachment occurrence, and actual immutable version/hash/
rendition if available. Supply page/span/time only when returned. Cite authorized
original message/file locators rather than generated summaries. Weak snippets
remain leads or explicitly limited observations. Missing evidence means “not
established in this window,” never false. Do not save quotes to files unless asked.

## Meeting preparation and identity review

For an already verified meeting ID, context has no person selector. Its minimum
acquisition allowance is 4096 bytes; skip if the grant is smaller. Include
transcript only when authorized, and reserve its excerpts within the same budget.

```bash
msgvault meetings context --id 801 --format json --max-bytes 4096 2>/dev/null | bounded 5000
msgvault meetings actions --person-id 42 --after 2026-08-08 --before 2026-11-06 --limit 2 --json 2>/dev/null | bounded 3000
msgvault identity matches show 9 --json 2>/dev/null | bounded 3000
```

Distinguish calendar event/RSVP from attendance, archived notes/transcripts from
their availability, and display-only speaker labels from confirmed identities.
Keep agenda/action suggestions separate from recorded commitments. No outreach,
task creation, calendar edits, enrollment, enrichment, sweep run or profile writes.
An identity decision is a separate operation requiring user confirmation of the
exact pair after fresh preview/token/revision inspection. A stale token needs a
new preview; accepted candidate state alone does not prove an applied merge.
Do not accept/reject/merge identities within this read-only workflow.

Return subject/window, sources and coverage/caps; a compact table of field,
current value, suggestion, evidence and status; then meeting context, identity
uncertainties and skipped coverage. State that no writes were performed. End
when the report or remaining budget is exhausted; no automatic follow-on work.

